The C program is a 33-line stdin→stdout echo built on a fixed [38;5;10mchar text[128][0m buffer. Two behaviors drive the output and are reproduced deliberately rather than replaced with a simple [38;5;10mio::copy[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mfgets[0m chunking**: at most 127 bytes are read per call, stopping right after a newline (which is retained). A final line lacking a newline is still returned; NULL comes back only when EOF hits with zero bytes read.[0m[0m
- **[38;5;10mfputs[0m NUL truncation**: [38;5;10mfputs[0m writes a C string, so a NUL byte embedded in the input silently discards the rest of that 127-byte chunk. This is the one case where the program is not a faithful echo, and it is preserved as-is per the no-bug-fixing requirement. Input [38;5;10mab\0cd\nnext\n[0m yields [38;5;10mabnext\n[0m from both binaries.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mfgets[0m is implemented over [38;5;10mBufRead::fill_buf[0m/[38;5;10mconsume[0m in safe Rust — no [38;5;10munsafe[0m, no raw buffers — with [38;5;10mEINTR[0m retried and read errors treated like EOF (matching [38;5;10mfgets[0m, which returns NULL on error unless bytes were already buffered). [38;5;10mfputs[0m's return value is ignored, as in the original.[0m[0m
